              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "COMMAND_INJECTION",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Potential Command Injection."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The highlighted API is used to execute a system command. If unfiltered input \nis passed to this API, it can lead to arbitrary command execution. \nVulnerable Code: \nimport java.lang.Runtime;\n\nRuntime r = Runtime.getRuntime();\nr.exec(\"/bin/sh -c some_tool\" + input);\nReferencesOWASP: Command InjectionOWASP: Top 10 2013-A1-InjectionCWE-78: \nImproper Neutralization of Special Elements used in an OS Command ('OS Command \nInjection')"
              },
              "messageStrings": {
                "default": {
                  "text": "This usage of {0} can be vulnerable to Command Injection."
                }
              },
              "helpUri": "https://find-sec-bugs.github.io/bugs.htm#COMMAND_INJECTION",
              "properties": {
                "tags": ["SECURITY"]
              },
              "relationships": [
                {
                  "target": {
                    "id": "78",
                    "guid": "de860c32-bc93-5e70-89d7-0877e5abaefb",
                    "toolComponent": {
                      "name": "CWE",
                      "guid": "b8c54a32-de19-51d2-9a08-f0abfbaa7310"
                    }
                  },
                  "kinds": ["superset"]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "PREDICTABLE_RANDOM",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Predictable pseudorandom number generator."
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The use of a predictable random value can lead to vulnerabilities when used in \ncertain security critical contexts. For example, when the value is used as: \n  * a CSRF token: a predictable token can lead to a CSRF attack as an attacker \n    will know the value of the token \n  * a password reset token (sent by email): a predictable password token can \n    lead to an account takeover, since an attacker will guess the URL of the \n    \"change password\" form \n  * any other secret value \n  \nA quick fix could be to replace the use of java.util.Random with something \nstronger, such as java.security.SecureRandom. \nVulnerable Code: \nString generateSecretToken() {\n    Random r = new Random();\n    return Long.toHexString(r.nextLong());\n}\nSolution: \nimport org.apache.commons.codec.binary.Hex;\n\nString generateSecretToken() {\n    SecureRandom secRandom = new SecureRandom();\n\n    byte[] result = new byte[32];\n    secRandom.nextBytes(result);\n    return Hex.encodeHexString(result);\n}\nReferencesCracking Random Number Generators - Part 1 (https://jazzy.id.au)CERT: \nMSC02-J. Generate strong random numbersCWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random \nValuesPredicting Struts CSRF Token (Example of real-life vulnerability and \nexploitation)"
              },
              "messageStrings": {
                "default": {
                  "text": "This random generator ({0}) is predictable."
                }
              },
              "helpUri": "https://find-sec-bugs.github.io/bugs.htm#PREDICTABLE_RANDOM",
              "properties": {
                "tags": ["SECURITY"]
              },
              "relationships": [
                {
                  "target": {
                    "id": "330",
                    "guid": "80cf7ee1-5a29-5fb4-a2f1-701272abf9b7",
                    "toolComponent": {
                      "name": "CWE",
                      "guid": "b8c54a32-de19-51d2-9a08-f0abfbaa7310"
                    }
                  },
                  "kinds": ["superset"]
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
